#{[17592186564454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ed337ad5-38b5-500f-9d10-ecd8816c9737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186564277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186564126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564459 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f15dd0ad-8bf1-5e1c-8d76-6465ccbd4d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope of the line $4x-2y=15?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01d6b59b-be2b-52a2-b7cf-129c1864b658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564464 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =15 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+15 \\\\ 2y & =4x-15 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{4x-15}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x}{2}-\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ y & =2x-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1071 "The lowest common denominator of $\\frac{5}{7}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "093f60b7-b595-5220-994a-63101bc04a42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564466 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators. \n\nSince the denominators $7$ and $2$ are prime numbers, they are already represented by their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down each prime factor, and we raise each factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is also $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${7}^{1}\\cdot {2}^{1}=14.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34054404a184f9480abec1692e343f7ffbae4664c9c906e744f07cc082b0ebde.png)\n\nWhat are the roots of the function $f(x)$ defined by the mapping diagram shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cdee882-c7e4-5094-92c4-9cde591d4ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564482 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots of the function are the values of $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In the given mapping diagram, we see that there is no value of $x$ that gives an output of zero. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has no roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f2dc2ed0562e3d93888ab025195ada5da5ef9bf5312d6215dfb83253c81f9be7.png)\n\nOrder the sides of $△KLM$ above from the shortest to the longest." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78772149-6410-5606-9ad3-23c6e01f9b0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564496 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $∠K$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{LM}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠M$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{KL}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nLM<KM<KL\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{LM},\\,\\overset{―}{KM},\\,\\overset{―}{KL}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c3a31aad745cafe59f0c15e56dd48bf9350e46c08e148b843a6904145934e46b.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ given the figure above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1a77f56-694e-539a-a24b-49a13b3865ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564513 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the two angles are supplementary, the sum of their measures is equal to ${180}^{∘}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{48}^{∘}+(x+{42}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $(x-3)^{4}=-16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b024a1aa-681f-5f33-860b-bc65702c1e19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564518 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $(x-3)^{4}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x$ because the exponent ($4$) is even. This expression can never be negative. \n\nTherefore, there are no real solutions to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60981" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 40%=32,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d64cbb84-4920-5e00-a5fb-dd325fcf710c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564526 #edb/ref 1074 "The part is $32,$ the percentage is $40%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =32÷40% \\\\  & =32÷\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =32\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2}\\times 5 \\\\  & =16\\times 5 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular nature reserve started with $100$ rabbits. The number of rabbits is expected to increase sixfold each decade. What equation should we solve to determine the number of decades it will take for the rabbit population to increase to $129\\,600?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d77788ff-b482-51d4-bdb1-79e3cff6db85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564528 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the number of rabbits increases sixfold every decade. So the number of rabbits, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $6.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of rabbits.\n\n- $b=6$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since the opening of the nature reserve.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=100\\cdot {6}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, to find the time it takes for the population to increase to $129\\,600,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =129\\,600 \\\\ 100\\cdot {6}^{t} & =129\\,600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1071 "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=2$ when $x=4,$ find the value of $y$ when $x=\\frac{4}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfc98e96-f028-5cd6-8e89-3ae59a19563f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564530 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=2$ when $x=4.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{4} \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & =k \\\\ 8 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{8}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =8÷\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{4} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186564391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-215145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $y$ where $-\\frac{1}{2y+7}=\\frac{4}{5y-2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8061560-b60f-50e8-a1e6-e8fcd0b1f879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564535 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $y=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{5}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2y+7} & =\\frac{4}{5y-2} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{2y+7} & =\\frac{4}{5y-2} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (5y-2) & =(2y+7)\\cdot 4 \\\\ -5y+2 & =8y+28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+2 & =8y+28 \\\\ -5y+2+5y & =8y+28+5y \\\\ 2 & =13y+28 \\\\ 2-28 & =13y+28-28 \\\\ -26 & =13y \\\\ \\frac{-26}{13} & =\\frac{13y}{13} \\\\ -2 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=-2$ is different from $y=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{5},$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $y=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564542 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564542 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d37e1d3-6c33-5f0f-bc28-e7b63d04fffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564542 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1945aad4-ec78-547d-9bd0-70ae80932ccf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47c4606f-8ebf-5865-94dc-f08b36d8f5af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c3b6142-2ace-585c-97b7-d4c65b594061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564563 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6082acad-1a61-5e48-a711-2234a35580ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564572 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "977c3dea-b742-55e2-995c-c851a738de08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b7559a0-2252-5bd4-af1e-a93265c6f941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af473a3b-d261-5719-88fc-cd279c0b70e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564585 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564585 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c892973b-5533-5fc4-ac18-c5f0e8662f83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564585 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186564530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186564588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186564592 #